    "OtherAbstract": {
      "Type": "PIP",
      "Language": "eng",
      "AbstractText": "Abortifacient effect of a single-dose, long-acting vaginal suppository containing prostaglandins (3 mg of 15-methyl PGF2a) was investigated in 104 early first trimester pregnancies. Termination was successful in 95 cases (91.3%). The abortion was considered uneventful and complete in 79 patients. In 16 successful cases, the course was complicated by prolonged, excessive bleeding and continued positive pregnancy tests which were negative at 6 weeks. Bleeding was considered by most patients to be heavier than normal menstruation. However, only 15% had a declined in hemoglobin concentrations of more than 10 g/liter. 9 cases were considered failures (8.7%). In patients with prolonged bleeding, substantial amounts of necrotic residual tissue were found in all patients curettaged 4 weeks after therapy. The decline of serum human chorionic gonadotropins and plasma progesterone levels was significantly slower in these patients as compared with uneventful abortions. High failure rate and bleeding problems necessitate administration of this agent clinically, although other agents may be suitable for personal administration with this vaginal technique."
    }
  },
